    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nFiscal year starts 1 July; all three date-derived values now work off it, keyed to the business-day-adjusted registration date.\n\n1. **`FiscalYear.java`** (new, small cohesive helper) \u2014 the one place that knows the fiscal-year rule:\n   - `of(date)` \u2014 rolls to a business day via the existing `BusinessDay`, then maps to the fiscal year (labelled by the calendar year it ends in: month \u2265 July \u2192 year + 1).\n   - `label(owner)` \u2014 `FY<YY>`.\n   - `tenure(owner)` \u2014 elapsed fiscal years between registration and today.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` DTO field (`FY<YY>`) and updated the now-stale `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 mapped `fiscalYear` to `FiscalYear.label`, and switched the `membershipNumber` year segment from the raw registration year to `FiscalYear.of(...)`.\n\n4. **`MembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 the tenure factor now checks `FiscalYear.tenure(owner) >= 1` instead of `> 365` days; removed the dead `tenureDays` helper and its imports.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\nThe fiscal-year logic lives entirely in one new small class with three trivial methods, so the two functions I touched in existing code stayed simple: `MembershipLevel.points` changed a single line (and shed a helper, lowering its class's complexity), and the mapper change is annotation-only. Nothing grew into a big method or leaked into a large class.\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: I labelled the fiscal year by its **ending** calendar year (so registration 2026-09-02 \u2192 `FY27`, membership `...-M27`). The spec fixes the format (`FY<YY>`) and the test only checks the pattern, but if you intended the **starting**-year convention (`FY26`), flip the `+ 1` in `FiscalYear.of` \u2014 tenure counting is unaffected either way.",
